  "live_records": {
    "q-105765": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105765",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨2,-1,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-1,1⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=6$ and $f(b)=2,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 2-6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,3⟩) \\\\ -4 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\ -4 & ={f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that ${f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=-4.$ *",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
