    "q-105744": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105744",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨3,-1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=4$ and $f(b)=-2,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ -2-4 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4⟩-⟨3,-1⟩) \\\\ -6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,5⟩ \\\\ -6 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
