    "q-105745": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105745",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨2,5⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-2⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=-3$ and $f(b)=5,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 5-(-3) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-2⟩-⟨2,5⟩) \\\\ 8 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-7⟩ \\\\ 8 & ={f}_{x}(c)-7{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-105745-a",
