    "q-106068": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106068",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨3,7⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨3,7⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,7)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,7⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ ({7}^{2}-{3}^{2})-({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2x,2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}39 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =⟨-2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,6⟩ \\\\ 39 & =-6{c}_{1}+12{c}_{2} \\\\ 13 & =-2{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{7-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{6} & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y-1 & =2x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $-2{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}=13,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+4(2{c}_{1}+1) & =13 \\\\ -2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{1}+4 & =13 \\\\ 6{c}_{1} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=2{c}_{1}+1=4.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},4).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
