    "q-106066": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106066",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=xy$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=xy$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ 2(4)-0(0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4⟩ \\\\ 8 & =2{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1} \\\\ 4 & ={c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{4-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $2{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}=8,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 4{c}_{1} & =4 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=2{c}_{1}=2.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(1,2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
